  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 0: Wavy line with a big LEWP (Line Echo Wave\nPattern) headed toward the CWA from the west. Expect it to\narrive shortly (5-6PM). SPC SVR Watch coordination call is\nlikely to occur soon. We were not included in the watch earlier.\nCan only imagine that was due to the uncertainty of the cloud\ncover clearing some and development of llvl instability. Too\nmuch cloud = too little CAPE/threat for severe gusts to reach\nthe ground. The forcing may not be enough to sustain the severe\nstorms too far eastward/into the mountains - it's still pretty \ncloudy in State College. We will see. At the least, the SW\n(Cambria and Somerset and perhaps all the counties in the \ncurrent Flash Flood Watch will have some threat of severe gusts.\nThere is a non-zero chance of tornadoes, too. Those would be\nalong the gust front/line. Cold pool is developing, so the line\nmay have that supporting the possible survival of the line into\nthe central and south-central mountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain from possible thunderstorms late this \nafternoon and evening could be locally very heavy over the \nLaurel Highlands and South-Central Mountains. There is still \npotential for some heavy rain to fall over the Lower Susquehanna\nValley and Central Ridge-and-Valley region.\n\nLine of storms moving in is moving steadily, quickly. The\nopportunities for flooding may, therefore, be decreased.\nHowever, the FFG in Somerset and portions of Cambria and nrn\nBedford counties is still hovering just above 1.2\". So, we can't\nlet our gaurd down as some of the radar estimates in PBZ's area\nhave been above that. The inflection point of the LEWP is the\nplace most likely to have the ability to exceed FFG as the rain\nin that feature could last just a little longer at any one \nloaction than the more-windy parts of the line. Despite the\ninstability showers and storms back in wrn OH, the only worry\nfor flooding and severe thunderstorms should be in the next \ncouple of hours.\n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure yields a short dry window for late \nMonday afternoon through Wednesday morning, before more storms \narrive Wednesday afternoon into Thursday.\n\nA cold front moves through Monday. Dewpoints don't drop much at\nall until later in the day Monday. So, there will still be \nplenty of time on Monday for instability to develop and pop \nshowers and perhaps a thunderstorm or two during the daytime. By\nsunset, these will be gone as dry air and loss of heating kill \nthem off. Dewpoints drop into the 50s for many Tuesday morning. \nLarge scale subsidence should yield a mostly sunny day, then. \nReturn flow Tuesday night and Wed will bring the humidity up and\na cold front will approach on Wednesday. Storms are expected \nmainly in the Wed-Wed night timeframe, but speed of the CFROPA \nwill determine if all/part of Thursday will be dry. If showers \nlinger, they would be over the south.",
